Let P be the given program.  We only need to exhibit one stable model (answer‐set) in which q ∈ X.

Since etrue is a fact, every candidate X contains etrue.  To make q true we need qcore in X, and qcore ← h0, so we must have h0 ∈ X.  Then a0 ← not h0,not h1 is removed and h0 ← not a0 forces a0 ∉ X, h0 ∈ X.

The remaining atoms split into four independent default‐cycles among pairs (h1,a1), (h2,a2), (h3,a3), (h4,a4), linked in a chain h1/a1 depends on h2, h2/a2 on h3, etc.  One can check systematically that there are in fact five answer‐sets containing h0 (and hence q):

 1. {h0,h1,h2,h3,h4,etrue,qcore,q}  
 2. {h0,h1,h2,h3,a4,etrue,qcore,q}  
 3. {h0,h1,h2,a3,a4,etrue,qcore,q}  
 4. {h0,h1,a2,a3,a4,etrue,qcore,q}  
 5. {h0,a1,a2,a3,a4,etrue,qcore,q}

Each of these satisfies all reduct‐based checks and contains q.  Hence q is true in at least one answer‐set.  

ANSWER: A

Let’s identify all candidate answer sets M, knowing s0 and s1 are facts and thus must be in every M. From s0 and s1 the rule “wide ← s0, s1” makes wide true in every M. So every candidate M has {s0,s1,wide} plus any subset of {p1,p0,q}.

1. List all models M of the program (i.e. sets closed under all rule heads whose bodies are satisfied in M):
   M must contain s0,s1,wide. Checking combinations for p1, p0, q, one finds five models:
     M2 = {s0,s1,wide,q}
     M3 = {s0,s1,wide,p0}
     M4 = {s0,s1,wide,p0,q}
     M7 = {s0,s1,wide,p1,p0}
     M8 = {s0,s1,wide,p1,p0,q}

2. For each model M, form the Gelfond–Lifschitz reduct P^M by:
    – removing r1 (q ← wide, not p0) entirely if p0∈M, or else replacing it by q ← wide;
    – keeping all other rules (none contain default negation).
   Then compute the least (ground) model of P^M and check if it equals M.

   – For M2 = {s0,s1,wide,q}, p0∉M2 so r1 becomes q ← wide. The least model of the reduct is exactly {s0,s1,wide,q} = M2. Hence M2 is a stable model.
   – For each of M3, M4, M7, M8, p0∈M so r1 is dropped. The reduct’s least model is {s0,s1,wide}, which is strictly smaller than M. Thus none of those is stable.

3. Conclusion: the only answer set is M2 = {s0,s1,wide,q}, in which q is true.

Since q is true in at least one (in fact the only) answer set, the credulous (brave) conclusion is that q definitely follows.

ANSWER: A
